  "hints": [
    "`int log[100]` runs out of room: one of the hidden logs has 400 readings. Ask for memory with malloc and grow it with realloc when it's full.",
    "Start with a small capacity, and when `count == capacity` double it: `int *bigger = realloc(log, capacity * sizeof(int));`. Check for NULL before using it.",
    "Printing backwards is `for (int i = count - 1; i >= 0; i--)`. Handle `count == 0` by printing `empty`, and free the block at the end."
  ],
  "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int capacity = 8, count = 0, value;\n    int *log = malloc(capacity * sizeof(int));\n    if (log == NULL) return 1;\n\n    while (scanf(\"%d\", &value) == 1 && value != 0) {\n        if (count == capacity) {\n            capacity *= 2;\n            int *bigger = realloc(log, capacity * sizeof(int));\n            if (bigger == NULL) {\n                free(log);\n                return 1;\n            }\n            log = bigger;\n        }\n        log[count++] = value;\n    }\n\n    if (count == 0) printf(\"empty\\n\");\n    for (int i = count - 1; i >= 0; i--) printf(\"%d\\n\", log[i]);\n    free(log);\n    return 0;\n}\n"
}
